## Backend Architecture Overview

Athena shall use a trusted server-side backend within the Next.js application boundary.

The backend shall coordinate all financially significant operations.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    Client[Authenticated Client]

    subgraph Backend["Trusted Backend Boundary"]
        Transport[Transport Layer]
        Application[Application Services]
        Domain[Domain Services]
        Repository[Repository Layer]
        Infrastructure[Infrastructure Adapters]
        Audit[Audit Service]
        Jobs[Background Jobs]
    end

    subgraph Data["Protected Data Boundary"]
        Database[(PostgreSQL)]
        Storage[(Private Object Storage)]
    end

    subgraph Identity["Identity Boundary"]
        Auth[Authentication Service]
    end

    Client --> Transport
    Transport --> Auth
    Transport --> Application
    Application --> Domain
    Application --> Repository
    Application --> Audit
    Application --> Jobs
    Domain --> Repository
    Repository --> Database
    Infrastructure --> Storage
    Jobs --> Application
```

The backend shall serve as the primary trust boundary between user-controlled input and authoritative financial records.

---

## Architectural Style

Athena's backend shall use:

- A modular monolith
- Layered responsibilities
- Domain-oriented organization
- Explicit command and query workflows
- Server-controlled authorization
- Deterministic financial logic
- Narrow persistence interfaces
- Auditable mutations
- Transactional database operations
- Replaceable infrastructure adapters

Athena shall not begin as a microservices system.

Independent services may be introduced later only when a documented operational or architectural requirement justifies the additional complexity.

---

## Backend Responsibilities

The backend is responsible for:

- Verifying authenticated identity
- Enforcing authorization
- Validating untrusted input
- Coordinating domain logic
- Persisting authoritative records
- Preserving import lineage
- Enforcing financial invariants
- Creating audit records
- Managing review workflows
- Generating trusted reports
- Supporting dashboard widgets
- Processing files
- Managing long-running work
- Returning safe responses
- Protecting secrets
- Preventing duplicate financial effects
- Recording failures and recovery status

The backend shall not delegate authoritative financial decisions to the browser.

---

## Trusted Execution Boundary

The trusted backend boundary includes:

- Server Components performing protected data retrieval
- Server Actions performing approved mutations
- Route Handlers exposing application interfaces
- Server-only service functions
- Database functions where explicitly approved
- Background jobs operating with restricted credentials
- Privileged storage operations

The following must remain outside the browser:

- Service-role credentials
- Privileged database access
- Authorization rules
- Financial calculations
- Import parsing and persistence
- Sensitive file access
- Audit creation
- Administrative operations
- Secret configuration

The browser must always be treated as untrusted.

---

## Request Processing Pipeline

Every protected backend request should follow a consistent sequence.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    Request[Incoming Request]
    Authenticate[Authenticate]
    Validate[Validate Input]
    Authorize[Authorize]
    Execute[Execute Application Workflow]
    Domain[Apply Domain Rules]
    Persist[Persist Transactionally]
    Audit[Create Audit Record]
    Respond[Return Safe Response]

    Request --> Authenticate
    Authenticate --> Validate
    Validate --> Authorize
    Authorize --> Execute
    Execute --> Domain
    Domain --> Persist
    Persist --> Audit
    Audit --> Respond
```

A request must not proceed to protected persistence unless authentication, validation, and authorization have succeeded.

